<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Ivory Coast</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Yamoussoukro</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A West African republic, independent from France in 1960, once a cocoa and coffee economic power and twice torn by civil war between 2002 and 2011.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-ivory-coast">French Ivory Coast</a>, a French colony since 1893, became an autonomous member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> on 4 December 1958 and achieved independence in 1960. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny">Félix Houphouët-Boigny</a>, the son of a Baoulé chief, became the first president and ruled until 1993.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Houphouët-Boigny's one-party government gave farmers good prices for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa, and the country became the world's leading cocoa producer by 1979. A recession and drought in the early 1980s damaged the economy, and strikes in 1990 forced the government to accept multi-party democracy. After Houphouët-Boigny died, Henri Konan Bédié promoted the concept of Ivoirité, which strained relations between groups. A military coup in 1999 put Robert Guéï in power, and Laurent Gbagbo became president in 2000.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>An armed uprising in September 2002 began the first civil war, which ended with a peace accord in March 2007. A disputed election in 2010 led to a second civil war, and Gbagbo was taken into custody on 11 April 2011. Alassane Ouattara has been president since 2010 and won a third term in November 2020, in elections the opposition boycotted. A new constitution was adopted in 2016.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ivory-coast.jpg" alt="Ivory Coast" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Côte_d'Ivoire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yamoussoukro</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Félix Houphouët-Boigny, Henri Konan Bédié, Robert Guéï, Laurent Gbagbo, Alassane Ouattara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, Christianity, traditional beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Dyula, Baoulé, Bété, Anyin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Côte d'Ivoire, Republic of Côte d'Ivoire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=ivory-coast" aria-label="Ivory Coast on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4534 1923 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ivory Coast lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4854 2179L4892 2234"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4892 2234h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4854 2179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4854 2179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yamoussoukro. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=ivory-coast">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
